Security review: the local dashboard's access control in the Deliberation repo (server/dashboard/, as on master at 4bf190b)

Question: is this access control adequate for what the dashboard serves, and what, if anything, must change before more users turn it on? Judge it against the threat model the repo states (quoted below), and say whether that model itself is the right one.

What the dashboard is: a separate local process (`deliberation-mcp dashboard`, `/deliberation:dashboard`) that tails the run journal every Deliberation MCP process writes, and serves it read-only to a browser: live and past runs as state graphs, config, provider health, stats and an analyzer. With `dashboard.capture: "metadata"` (default) it serves no prompt or response text; with `"content"` it serves prompts and model answers (secrets scrubbed at write time). PII is redacted at serve time unless `dashboard.showPII` is on.

Threat model as written in docs/dev/design-decisions.md (verbatim):

> Threat model: loopback bind, Host/Origin pinned to `127.0.0.1:<port>`/`localhost:<port>` against DNS rebinding, a per-start 32-byte token in the printed URL swapped for an `HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict` cookie (browsers send it to every 127.0.0.1 port: Host/Origin checks and `nosniff` stop a web page from reading data cross-port, but a local service that receives the cookie can replay the token server-side, e.g. curl with `Host: 127.0.0.1:<port>` plus the cookie or `?t=`, and read all dashboard data. Accepted because any local listener is already inside the same-machine trust boundary - the design scope is a single-user laptop; `dlb_dash` is only issued after the user opens the tokenized URL, and a restart rotates the token. On shared multi-user machines, do not enable the dashboard or `capture: "content"`), the token kept off argv via a 0600 redirect file, and CSP + `textContent`-only rendering.

Routes after the guard (all GET/HEAD): `/` (UI), `/assets/*` (static, realpath-confined to the UI dir), `/api/runs`, `/api/runs/<id>`, `/api/config`, `/api/health`, `/api/stats`, `/api/analyzer`, `/api/events` (SSE stream of journal events).

