=== Proposed changes after round 1 (the code above is unchanged; this is the remediation plan under review) ===

1. Split the one token into two secrets. At start the server draws a one-time exchange code (32 random bytes) and, per browser session, a session secret (32 random bytes). `?t=<code>` is accepted once, and only within 10 minutes of start; a second use or a late one gets 401. The exchange response sets no cookie: it redirects to `/#s=<session secret>`. The fragment never reaches a server or a Referer.
2. Header-only credential. `/` and `/assets/*` (the UI's static files, the same code as the public repo) are served without a credential. A boot script from `/assets/` reads the fragment, stores the secret in `sessionStorage`, and removes it from the address bar and history with `history.replaceState`. Every `/api/*` route requires `X-Dashboard-Session: <secret>`; no cookie is set or read. A service on another port never receives the secret, and a page on another port cannot read this origin's sessionStorage.
3. SSE. `EventSource` cannot send headers, so `/api/events` takes `?ticket=`: a single-use, 30-second ticket the UI fetches from `GET /api/ticket` with the header. A used or expired ticket gets 401.
4. Lifetime and revocation. A session secret expires after 8 hours idle. `GET /api/logout` with the header revokes it. A restart drops all sessions and draws a new exchange code.
5. Output. stdout and the host transcript still get the URL, but it carries only the one-time code, which is dead after first use or after 10 minutes. The 0600 opener page is deleted as soon as the code is used, or at 10 minutes, so a crash leaves at most a dead code on disk. A new code needs `/deliberation:dashboard restart`.
6. Unchanged: loopback bind, Host and Origin pinned to the bound port, GET/HEAD only, CSP `default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'`, nosniff, no-referrer, textContent-only rendering, constant-time comparisons for the code, the secret and tickets.
7. Threat model rewrite in docs/dev/design-decisions.md and TECHNICAL.md: other local listeners, other OS users and containers on the same loopback are NOT inside the trust boundary; the design protects data from them as long as they cannot read the user's files or browser storage. Supported: a single-user machine with direct loopback access. Not supported: shared multi-user machines, reverse proxies, tunnels and port forwarding (the Host pin refuses most of them). On Windows the state file and opener page rely on the per-user profile ACLs, since 0600 is not enforced. `capture: "content"` and `showPII` say so in their schema descriptions.
8. Tests: a second use of the code and a late use both get 401; `/api/*` without the header gets 401 and with a cookie only still gets 401; a ticket works once; idle expiry and logout; the opener page is gone after the exchange; Host and Origin tests unchanged.

Out of scope (deferred): refusing to start `capture: "content"` on a detected multi-user machine; that needs a reliable detection method of its own.

