=== Proposed changes, revised after round 2 (the code above is unchanged; this is the remediation plan) ===

1. Two secrets, memory only. At start the server draws a one-time exchange code (32 random bytes); each exchange mints a session secret (32 random bytes). Neither is written to the state file, which keeps only pid, port, argv and procStartedAt for `--stop`. Duplicate-start detection probes a new unauthenticated `GET /api/identity` (returns the pid and a per-start public id, no data), so no probe ever presents the code.
2. Exchange. `/?t=<code>` serves the static UI shell with `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`; its boot script removes `?t=` from the address bar and history with `history.replaceState` before anything else, then calls `GET /api/exchange?t=<code>`, which consumes the code before it responds (a second or parallel request gets 401; tested in parallel) and returns `{ secret }` as a no-store JSON body. The secret never appears in a URL. The code also expires 10 minutes after it is minted.
3. Header-only credential. The secret is kept in `localStorage` (scoped to this origin and port, so pages on other ports cannot read it, and new tabs of the dashboard share it). Every `/api/*` route except `/api/identity` and `/api/exchange` requires `X-Dashboard-Session`; no cookie is set or read, and query credentials are refused on every route but `/api/events`.
4. SSE. `/api/events` accepts only `?ticket=`: 32 random bytes from `GET /api/ticket` (header required), bound to that session, single use, 30-second lifetime, never logged. The UI does not use native EventSource retry: on error it mints a new ticket and reconnects with the last event id as `since`.
5. Lifetime and revocation. A session expires after 8 hours without an authenticated request; an open stream does not refresh it. `GET /api/logout` (header required) and idle expiry revoke the session, invalidate its unused tickets and end its open streams. A restart drops everything.
6. Re-entry. `deliberation-mcp dashboard --code` asks the running process for a fresh one-time code over a 0600 Unix domain socket (a named pipe on Windows) in the 0700 state dir, writes a new 0600 opener page and opens it. The 401 page says to run it. stdout carries only one-time codes, never a session secret.
7. Unchanged: loopback bind, Host and Origin pinned to the bound port, GET/HEAD only, CSP `default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'`, nosniff, no-referrer, textContent-only rendering, constant-time comparisons.
8. Threat model rewrite in docs/dev/design-decisions.md and TECHNICAL.md as in round 1 (other local listeners, OS users and containers are outside the trust boundary; no shared machines, proxies, tunnels or port forwarding; Windows relies on profile ACLs), plus README.md, SETUP.md, docs/tool-guide.md and commands/dashboard.md for the new flow and `--code`.
9. Tests: parallel exchange gives one secret; late and reused codes get 401; no secret on disk or in any URL; `/api/*` without the header gets 401, a cookie alone gets 401, a query credential off `/api/events` gets 401; a valid ticket without the header works once on `/api/events` only; logout and idle expiry end an open stream and void unused tickets; reconnect mints a new ticket and resumes from the last id; `--code` works while running; the identity probe needs no secret.

